book_text = "ON THE DIMMING, AND WHETHER IT IS OVER
by Magister Ysolt of the Glass College, in the 899th year of the Arrival

It is commonly said that the Dimming ended in the 760s, when the colleges stopped counting the fallen towers. I have spent eleven years measuring what the stones of the twelve wells give, and I must report that it did not end. It slowed.

Every stone I have measured gives less than its own records say it gave a century ago. Not much less. A little, every year, the way a well gives a little less in a dry summer, except that the summers have not been dry.

Before the Dimming, masters crossed the Empire in a day. Caelmere stood in the air above the Riverlands until 731, and a city does not fall out of the sky because its builders grew careless. It falls because what held it up was spent.

My conclusion is that the residue is a well, not a spring. What was given to us at the Arrival was given once, and we have been drawing on it for nine hundred years.

I have been told this conclusion is unhelpful.

I am told the same of the question that follows from it: if it was given once, who gave it, and did they mean to?"
